> 
> But we're not talking about "each incident".

You've introduced a BoH explosion as reasoning for having more candles
be generated in the game. It is not a sensible game design decision to
answer this or other incidents with just providing more of the destroyed
resources in more places in the game or arbitrarily increase their
generation probabilities. In my book, good design looks different.[*]

[*] See TJR's suggestions in this thread for examples.

> Candles are necessary to win the game and a really rare resource in
> Vanilla.

Yes. And if you have destroyed your candles just before the incantation,
that incident should not be compensated by the game providing arbitrary
amounts of something that is, obviously, even intended to be rare.

> The only guaranteed source is Izchak's shop and even there you might
> not get enough and then Minetown is rather early in the game whereas
> you need the candles at the end. Lots of possibilities of accidents
> with or without the player's fault.

(IME, typically the player's fault. But, so what?)

> The duration of the game between castle and the invocation is too long

"too long"? - The whole game is "too long" because many thousands of turns
makes it more likely that one dies or loses all or part of ones equipment.

No one prevents a player from not picking up the candles in the first
place, rather to come back to get them immediately before the incantation.
No one prevents a player from picking up the candles and depositing them
in a chest of his stash.

> already. When everything else is ready and you're standing on the VS
> sport, having to spent time getting an item that is only useful just
> for this one moment(*), that is annoying and no fun at all.

IMO, it's no fun if the game is forgiving and compensating every mistake.

I've once blown up my BoH (including the candles) just before I wanted
to enter the Fake Tower to start my late game run. So what? I had to pay
for my mistake and re-build my whole ascension kit, not only the candles.
It's not the game's task to compensate a player's fault in any way.[**]

[**] Rather Nethack's recover philosophy is mostly a "slow-forgiving" one.

> The current solution is a probably temporary quick-fix, but one that
> fits well into the game: "Gnomes in mines with candles".

I cannot see how that would "fit well" into the game.

> * Because of its scarcity candles are de facto an one-usage-item which
>   goes against the spirit of NetHack.

Beginners may even waste the candles to have some light in the mines to
increase their survival probability. So what?

